WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.770
- So my name is Julia Istomina.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.280
I am the Associate Director
of the Graduate Writing Lab

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:13.610
at Yale Poorvu Center for
Teaching and Learning.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:16.050
I also administer the Poorvu Center

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.670
Public Communication Certificate program

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:23.080
for Graduate School Arts
and Sciences students

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.980
who compete in a Three
Minute Thesis competition.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.750
So we're really excited to
offer this to those people

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:30.190
who are competing in the 3MT

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.040
and to anyone doing online presentations,

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.640
which is literally everyone right now.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.760
I'll also add before I do the introduction

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:41.110
of our wonderful speaker and expert,

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:44.560
that they were training
us to do interviews

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.060
by Skype seven years ago.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.430
This topic was always relevant.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.780
I think that online
workshops and presentations

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:54.750
and interviews are not gonna go away.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.540
So I think that these are
great resources to put

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.620
in your back pocket,
whether you're doing the 3MT

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.290
or some kind of really
awesome presentation

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:05.123
of your research,

00:01:05.123 --> 00:01:07.730
or if you're doing more
kind of mundane job

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:10.160
or get things and other conferences.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.360
So a very special person is
with us today, Guy Ortoleva.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.600
I'm going to make his introductions.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.450
Before I do, please make
sure that your mic is muted

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:20.750
and that your picture is off.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:23.600
In that way, your image will
not appear in the recording.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:27.080
For questions, what we're gonna do

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.380
is we're gonna have like a 15
minute Q and A at one o'clock

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.230
or a little bit before.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:34.580
So if you have a question
that you'd like to ask of Guy,

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.010
please chat it and I will
keep track of them, okay?

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.210
So I will call on your first name

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.290
during the Q and A session.

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:44.820
So Guy Ortoleva, on behalf
of the Yale Broadcast Studio,

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.890
is going to help you
improve your presentations

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:51.540
with a tool kit of communication
and technical tips.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.160
Guy and his team, I'll say right now,

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.860
they're all at the forefront
of all of the communications

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.100
that happen at the university.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.250
So they're there front
and center for graduation,

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.010
for matriculation, for all
the ceremony, they capture it.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.590
They share so many things publicly

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.450
and within the universities.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.250
So these are the professionals,

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.910
they're the professionals you go to

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:14.160
for really quality works.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.890
So I'm just really grateful for Guy's time

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.810
and the work that he's put
into this presentation.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.710
To let you know a little
bit more about him.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:24.830
Guy has worked in the Broadcast
Studio for several years

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:27.520
and both records and does video editing.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.740
Before that, he worked
on many television shows,

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.790
like "Inside Edition" "and Hard Copy",

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.870
documentaries, commercials
and corporate training videos.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:37.890
He has a JD, but more importantly,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:42.040
he has 30 years of experience
in communicating with video.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.520
So I'm gonna mute myself

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.960
and give the mic over to Guy, as it were.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.693
So thank you so much
and welcome everybody.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:53.903
- Hello, thank you for attending.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:57.590
Just remember, you can put
your questions in the chat.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.210
I did have quite a few
years working in television,

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:08.210
but for the past two years
at Yale, since COVID,

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:12.070
much of my time is
looking at Zoom recordings

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:15.140
from all around the world
and all the guest lecturers,

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:18.200
and then editing them and
trying to put them on YouTube

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:21.160
and wherever they're going to go.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:22.430
I've seen a lot.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:26.640
So hopefully, today I can show
you how to improve your image

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:27.480
on Zoom.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.010
And then specifically,
we'll talk about the 3MT,

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.133
types of recordings.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.760
When asked why the show, "60 Minutes",

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.680
was so successful for so many years,

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:45.340
executive producer, Don Hewitt
said, "tell me a story."

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:46.850
And that's pretty much it.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:50.650
The success of that show has
been that every Sunday night,

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.790
they tell you a story.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:56.000
That's what you're doing
when you're doing your 3MT

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.840
or anytime you're really
doing a presentation on Zoom.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.190
You have to have a beginning,

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.103
you have to have a middle and an end.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:10.060
After we've gotten MTV in the '80s,

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.160
everything happens a lot faster.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.820
I think it's very important
for you to get the attention

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.530
of your audience in the first 15 seconds,

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:18.363
give them a reason

00:04:18.363 --> 00:04:20.690
to really continue
paying attention to you.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:22.610
And then at the end,

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:25.233
you wanna wrap it up
with some real impact.

00:04:26.641 --> 00:04:29.520
In the middle, you're
providing all of the content.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.230
Again, you wanna hold
people's attention throughout.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:33.523
You have to be dynamic.

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:40.170
I found what was helpful in
planning for any presentation

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.570
that I do, that I have a
one sentence objective.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:46.440
Can I sum up what I
want the takeaway to be?

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:50.250
What I want people to
leave with in one sentence.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:52.740
And then as I prepare my material,

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.510
I hold it up to that one sentence.

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:57.113
Nice, is this a need to
know or a nice to know?

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.760
One of the things to keep in
mind when you're presenting

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.640
on camera and/or if you have slides,

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.360
or however you're presenting it,

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.650
people can assimilate
one thing or another.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:13.150
They can't assimilate your sound

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.660
and a slide at the same time.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.440
The balance is gonna tip
one way or the other.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.420
So you've got something
really compelling to say,

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:23.980
you don't want the
slide to be distracting.

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:25.890
If the slide has the information

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:28.183
that you really need to be
showing, well, keep in mind

00:05:28.183 --> 00:05:29.960
that people are gonna
be looking at the slide

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:32.823
and your voice is gonna be
kind of in the background.

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.750
What people are seeing
of you has to be useful.

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:41.860
Be careful of your background material,

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:42.920
be careful of your clothing.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:45.290
We'll talk about clothing later.

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:49.380
But everything that's in
the frame has to be useful

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:52.800
and not detract from you.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.780
So framing, lighting and audio

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:58.660
is how you successfully tell a story.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:00.610
That's what we're going to cover today.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.540
Proper framing really is, for example,

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.440
I'm in a fairly balanced view.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.254
You can see me pretty well.

00:06:10.254 --> 00:06:11.854
I have a little bit of headroom.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.910
Background is totally
in the falling in a way,

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.350
you don't really notice it.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:19.410
The lighting's pretty good.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.450
You can see my eyes.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:23.373
The audio quality is pretty good.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:26.980
People, you can understand
what I'm saying.

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:31.890
The framing, the lighting and
the audio is really important.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.440
Everything else is nice,

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:38.420
but if you fail any of those
above, you're in trouble.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:40.340
Again, we're gonna discuss
the three minute thesis

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.240
specific things a little bit later on.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:47.773
This fellow, Jeff Carlson,
kindly did a number of images.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:52.350
This is what I see with so
many different Zoom meetings.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.503
You have a variety of different images.

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:59.590
The first one is that not enough
headroom or the up to nose.

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:01.750
This is the same thing
with opening your laptop.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:03.800
We're looking up at you,

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:06.000
but you're cutting the
top of your head off.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:09.120
You wanna avoid looking up so much.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.930
You're seeing the ceiling
and all that other stuff.

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:14.113
Second image, too much back light.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:16.730
That's where you have a window

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:19.520
or a bright light source behind you.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:20.770
As we'll talk about later,

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:22.880
the camera averages the amount of light.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:25.740
So if you've got a lot of
bright light behind you,

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:28.240
your face is going to be dark.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:30.760
Or worse, if you have like a window

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.430
and you start moving your head one way,

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:33.750
the camera will open up,

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:36.490
you get that almost pulsing
lighting look of the camera

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:40.323
will open up the iris and
then close it down quickly.

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:44.340
A plain white background is not so great.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.530
It kinda looks like a mugshot,

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:48.703
which you don't wanna really do.

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:52.110
And then when you have too much headroom,

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:54.900
this is the other example
I use with Julia there.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.230
Too much headroom is not so good.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:59.050
You've all seen the movie, "Alien".

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:01.173
You've probably seen lots of scary movies.

00:08:02.336 --> 00:08:04.430
When the music starts playing

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:08.050
and you just know something
bad is gonna happen,

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:11.180
in "Alien", the monster
comes down from above.

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:12.740
Well, the reason you know that

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:15.080
is because they've added
a little extra room

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:18.270
in the direction of when the
bad thing is about to happen.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:20.930
So I've always said that when
you have too much headroom,

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:23.620
that's because there's gonna
be a monster descending

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:24.453
upon you.

00:08:24.453 --> 00:08:26.013
So be careful with extra headroom.

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:28.053
Busy background.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:31.760
There's lots of kind of busy backgrounds.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:35.900
One of the ones that I see
a lot of are bookcases.

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:38.500
What happens, you've got a busy background

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.990
and after a while, your voice drones on

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:43.900
and people are really reading
the spines of the titles

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:44.870
in the books behind you.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:47.000
They wanna see what you're reading.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:48.800
They're not listening to you anymore.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:50.440
So when you have a busy background,

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:53.450
keep in mind that is
going to be distracting.

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:54.700
What's the main topic?

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:55.533
You.

00:08:55.533 --> 00:08:58.040
You wanna avoid distracting people.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:01.010
Of course, in the bottom
right is the good balance

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:02.273
that we were looking for.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:08.340
Before I go on to any kind of
a Zoom meeting, any of them,

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:09.980
I open up my Zoom Preferences

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:12.150
and I take a look at what I look like

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:17.150
and make sure that I have my
background is relatively nice.

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:20.123
Make sure my camera's on HD.

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:23.110
And then I also go to the audio

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:26.390
and make sure that I've got
a reasonably good audio.

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:30.010
The green bar, when you do a test audio,

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:32.570
should go about halfway to
three quarters of the way

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:33.990
to the right.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:37.610
That's gonna give you
really pretty good sound.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:39.710
I do this before anytime I go on

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:41.650
and I encourage you to do the same thing,

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:44.350
because you wanna see what you look like

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:47.340
and you wanna also make sure
that your audio is good.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:48.993
So do this before each meeting.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.780
What people will see.

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:56.403
There's a rule of thumb in video editing,

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:00.010
in any kind of thing with camera,

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:03.830
but it's built-in to our
DNA that the first thing

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:07.640
that people will be looking
for when they look at a frame

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.390
are other people's eyes.

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:10.783
It's just natural.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:15.020
The next thing they'll look for is motion.

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:16.910
If there's anything motion in the frame.

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:18.870
And then after they've
satisfied themselves

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:20.650
with the eyes and any motion,

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:23.420
they're gonna start looking
around for the brightest light.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:25.420
And then finally, details.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:27.163
But I'll go back to the eyes.

00:10:28.204 --> 00:10:32.190
I remember years ago, my
son had a lizard, (chuckles)

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:34.600
little chameleon, cute little thing.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:38.290
But anytime you walk by the
room or walked in the room

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:42.343
or was anywhere around him, he
looked you right in the eyes.

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:45.670
All animals, all creatures,

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:50.070
all people will first look into your eyes.

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:52.580
That's important because
you gotta make sure

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:54.360
that your eyes are well-enough lit

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:56.110
and that they're large enough in the frame

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:57.760
that people are gonna see them.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.833
Otherwise, they're
gonna be looking around.

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:03.890
The next thing is also your body language.

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:08.630
People tend to gesture
your hand out like that,

00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:09.930
which is great.

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:12.380
If you're dynamic enough
speaker, a lot of times,

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:13.860
people will be going like this

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:16.920
and you'll be carrying people's attention.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:20.390
But your body language has to be useful.

00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:23.190
This doesn't help.

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:24.840
You're putting up a barrier.

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:27.750
But your body language
is an important part

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.560
and as well as how you look.

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:31.540
We'll cover clothing later.

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:35.410
But it's your visual presentation
of what you look like

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:37.223
on camera that says a lot.

00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:41.810
Also, are you going to be
in the frame with the slide?

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:44.500
I know with the 3MT, you're using Panopto.

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:46.790
So you're gonna be side along side

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:49.483
to the left side of the slide.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:52.910
In Zoom, a lot of times,
if you've got slides,

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:55.340
you're gonna be the
upper right hand corner.

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:58.043
So you've gotta anticipate
how that will look.

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:03.110
The best thing is to have frame
yourself up with a closeup

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:07.100
or what we call a medium
closeup, not your entire body,

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:09.000
because again, the eyes,

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.800
which is what people are
gonna be looking for,

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:12.560
you want them to be seen.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:14.640
And then whatever slides you have,

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:16.140
well, they've gotta be telling a story

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:18.900
and they should amplify what
it is that you're saying,

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:20.950
because remember, it's your spoken word

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:23.530
and how you make your presentation

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:24.830
that really carries the day.

00:12:24.830 --> 00:12:28.223
The slide is just amplifying
it or reinforcing you.

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:32.790
Speaking of slides, you gotta
make sure that any images

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:34.870
or video clips, or anything you have,

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:36.520
have been cleared with copyright.

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:40.910
You can easily get stuff
from Wikimedia comments

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:44.023
and other places, or
you can ask permission.

00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:48.350
The best thing to do is
get these things clear,

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:52.250
because your video, your
presentation may end up on YouTube.

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:55.470
If it does, they'll pick it off

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:59.363
if you're borrowing somebody's
material without permission.

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:04.370
When you're speaking to an audience,

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:06.150
you gotta be on the same level.

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:07.240
I mean, you don't like it

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:09.570
when you have to look up at somebody.

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:11.960
Just opening your laptop
and starting to talk

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:13.400
doesn't cut it anymore.

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:14.940
You really have to put yourself

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:17.053
at the same level as the camera.

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:21.760
You should be lit twice as
bright as your background,

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:24.320
and that will create a
separation from your background.

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:27.230
So you're gonna have to probably
use some kind of lights,

00:13:27.230 --> 00:13:28.700
either daylight coming in,

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:31.380
or maybe a light on
the table or something.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:33.810
That light should be near the lens.

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:36.030
So it's not casting big shadows,

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:37.873
but it's not blinding you either.

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:41.590
If you're lost in the
framing, in other words,

00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:45.970
if you've chosen to show all
of your body or most of it

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:48.630
rather than a medium closeup or a closeup,

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:50.660
it's gonna take away from your story.

00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:52.150
I'm here to tell you.

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:54.100
Especially if they put
you up in the corner

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:57.310
or along the side, when
you're showing some slides,

00:13:57.310 --> 00:13:59.010
you're gonna be lost in the frame.

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:01.173
And audio.

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:05.820
You gotta do the audio well enough,

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:08.040
so that people really understand you.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:10.880
You can get headsets,
you can use your earbuds,

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:14.410
or best of all, use some kind
of a cardioid microphone,

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:16.520
something that's directional.

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:19.070
The microphone should be no more than two

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:20.650
or three feet away.

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:21.903
I always say an iPhone sounds great

00:14:21.903 --> 00:14:24.437
as long as you have it close enough.

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:28.520
Choosing a background can be as simple

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:30.853
as opening up your laptop,

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:34.950
getting up your Zoom
image so you see yourself

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:37.820
and then walk around to
different places in your house.

00:14:37.820 --> 00:14:39.620
Find a good background.

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:42.070
Something that has some nice, soft light

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:45.020
or available light coming in
so it's lighting your face,

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:46.400
has a pleasing background.

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:49.060
But move around, find the best place.

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:50.883
You might find two or three places.

00:14:51.770 --> 00:14:54.240
I know my wife unfortunately
took the best place,

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:55.300
she does telemedicine.

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:59.130
So I found myself down in the
basement doing my Zoom events.

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:02.710
But find yourself with a good background.

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:06.303
Windows can hurt you if
you've got them behind you.

00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:10.980
You wanna avoid large flat surfaces,

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:12.893
that will start reflecting the sound,

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:16.580
more of a cluttered room,
at least on the side

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:19.853
so we don't see it, can help
break up some of that sound.

00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:25.310
Two most common errors in camera work.

00:15:25.310 --> 00:15:27.370
The low angle shot.

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:29.040
One of my colleagues is along here

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:31.300
in the upper left-hand corner image.

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:35.730
You see, his eyeline is above the camera.

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:39.040
This happens typically,
people open up their laptop,

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:41.290
bring themselves on a Zoom
meeting and start talking.

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:44.193
So you're kind of looking up their nose.

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:48.723
And then you've got too much headroom.

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:55.913
So the headroom, you gotta
just tilt to monitor down.

00:15:57.240 --> 00:15:58.820
And then, of course, the bottom right,

00:15:58.820 --> 00:16:01.283
we have an example of proper headroom.

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:04.670
Back to framing.

00:16:04.670 --> 00:16:07.640
We do in television something
called rule of sixes.

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:12.513
We divided image up with
six lines and six areas.

00:16:13.423 --> 00:16:17.670
This image of me (laughs)
kinda shows the way

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:19.800
you wanna balance yourself out in a frame,

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.150
if you're looking straight forward.

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:26.403
You have a little bit of
headroom, not too much underneath.

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:29.430
This is what's called a medium closeup,

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:31.110
and that's probably your best bet.

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:33.360
Your eyes are gonna be visible enough.

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:36.100
I have one of these backgrounds,

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:37.420
'cause again, I'm downstairs

00:16:37.420 --> 00:16:40.130
and I don't wanna show what's behind me,

00:16:40.130 --> 00:16:42.640
so I use one of these virtual backgrounds.

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:45.890
But what I do is throw it
out of focus a little bit.

00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:49.190
So put it in Photoshop, soften it up.

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:51.720
In that way, it's not distracting

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:55.933
or at least hopefully not
distracting from the speaker.

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:02.220
Now the alternative
framing is, as mentioned,

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:04.183
if you've got slides,

00:17:05.340 --> 00:17:08.721
the person's gonna be in
the upper right-hand corner.

00:17:08.721 --> 00:17:12.550
That shows me, I'm
probably blocking Julia.

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:16.460
But if I'm looking at one side
and I'm looking over here,

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:18.030
I'm looking out of frame.

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:21.770
So you wanna be looking in
the direction of your slides,

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:24.290
because if you're looking in
the direction of the slides,

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:26.010
if somebody's looking at your eyes,

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:28.710
their eyes are gonna
be led back into frame

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:32.540
where the slides are and
paying attention to the slides.

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:35.410
But if you're looking out of
frame, well, what happens?

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:38.500
People look at your eyes and they're lost.

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:39.500
They're not looking anywhere.

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:44.190
So keep in mind, when you're
doing a long presentation,

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:46.080
you may wanna have a script there.

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:50.160
You could put it in the
upper part of your frame,

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:52.900
but just make sure you
put it in the proper side,

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:56.353
so that you're not inadvertently
looking out a frame.

00:17:57.190 --> 00:17:59.313
Remember the eyes are the key.

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:02.300
People are gonna look in your eyes.

00:18:02.300 --> 00:18:04.260
Again, when you're in a small frame

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:07.790
in the upper right hand
corner of a Zoom image,

00:18:07.790 --> 00:18:11.060
or even on the 3MTs, when
you're off to the side,

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:14.150
you're a smaller picture out of necessity.

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:17.440
So you wanna have that medium closeup shot

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:18.903
so that we can see you,

00:18:20.030 --> 00:18:22.230
'cause your face is
what's really important.

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:27.020
But avoid drawing attention
away from the subject matter,

00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:29.293
which is you and/or your slides.

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:32.850
So how does a camera see?

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:37.780
Well it sees like we do, it has an iris.

00:18:37.780 --> 00:18:41.040
So in this image, when you
have a little bright light,

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:42.860
you squint a little bit,

00:18:42.860 --> 00:18:46.910
or your iris will open up
to bring in more light.

00:18:46.910 --> 00:18:48.820
A camera is pretty much the same,

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:52.240
only it's not quite the same.

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:55.320
It's not adjustable infinitely.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:59.240
It adjusts to the limit of its technology,

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:02.890
but it has something called auto-iris.

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:05.430
It averages the amount of light.

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:10.430
So in a situation where you
have bright light behind you,

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:13.890
the camera is going to
average the amount of light

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:16.220
and it's going to close its iris down,

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:17.820
because it thinks it's
got plenty of light,

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:19.763
but what happens is now you're darker.

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:24.700
When technology averages,
it's not so good.

00:19:24.700 --> 00:19:29.290
So for example, if your head is in an oven

00:19:29.290 --> 00:19:33.040
and your feet are in the
freezer, a computer might say,

00:19:33.040 --> 00:19:35.650
on average, that person
is quite comfortable.

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:39.653
So averaging in technology
isn't always your best friend.

00:19:41.458 --> 00:19:45.103
It also happens when you have color.

00:19:46.592 --> 00:19:49.740
One of the questions that
people will ask me sometimes

00:19:49.740 --> 00:19:53.400
when they've seen themselves
on a Zoom is, why am I orange?

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:55.110
Why am I orange?

00:19:55.110 --> 00:19:59.240
Well, it happens because
the camera is averaging,

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:01.900
not just the amount of light

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:06.080
but it's looking at the kind
of light or the color of light.

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:08.610
So in other words, if you have daylight,

00:20:08.610 --> 00:20:11.180
or in the surface, a lot of white walls

00:20:11.180 --> 00:20:13.900
with having daylight reflecting off of it,

00:20:13.900 --> 00:20:17.200
the camera thinks that,
boom, this is more outside.

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:19.150
So it's going to adjust itself

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:22.060
to one end of the color spectrum.

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:25.740
If you have an incandescent
light on your desk,

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:27.660
your face is gonna be orange.

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:32.570
Quick simple solution for this
is to buy some daylight bulbs

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:33.793
to put in your lamp.

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:38.750
What about this color difference?

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:42.380
If you remember the old
electromagnetic spectrum

00:20:42.380 --> 00:20:45.720
from back in your science class days,

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:48.210
the visible spectrum is from the low end,

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:51.860
which is the reds all
the way up to your blues.

00:20:51.860 --> 00:20:54.320
So a candle light is at the one end

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:57.560
of low end of the
spectrum, it's very warm.

00:20:57.560 --> 00:21:00.360
Your incandescent bulbs
that most people have

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:04.360
in their houses are in the
low end of the spectrum.

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:08.820
We measure color temperature
in kelvins, or K, we call it.

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:12.610
So for example, your
typical bulb that you have

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:17.610
in your house, your soft white
bulb is around 2,600 kelvin,

00:21:18.460 --> 00:21:21.030
2,600K, we call it.

00:21:21.030 --> 00:21:25.233
Daylight, on the other hand,
is probably around 56K.

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:31.070
A screaming blue sky
could be maybe 10,000K.

00:21:31.070 --> 00:21:34.070
So it's way up in the
blue end of the spectrum.

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:35.680
So if let's say, you have a situation

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:40.020
where you've got some nice
soft daylight coming in,

00:21:40.020 --> 00:21:45.020
but you also have a lamp on
helping to illuminate your face,

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:49.460
you wanna make sure that
the two color temperatures

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:51.250
are not fighting one another.

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:56.250
So if the camera looks at
the light and averages it

00:21:56.990 --> 00:22:00.360
and it says, gee, we're at
the blue end of the spectrum

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:03.350
and you put some orange
light on your face,

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:04.380
you're gonna look orange,

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:06.950
your background is gonna look fine.

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:10.150
So again, that's where the
daylight bulb as an option

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:13.083
for your light fixtures will come in.

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:19.373
The other opposite end happens too.

00:22:20.490 --> 00:22:23.580
There have been a few times
that I turn on my camera

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:25.030
and I'm blue.

00:22:25.030 --> 00:22:25.863
Why?

00:22:25.863 --> 00:22:30.610
Because I overcompensated
and I had a blue shirt on.

00:22:30.610 --> 00:22:35.610
So sometimes, the cameras will
pick up on your clothing too

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:39.150
and say, oh gee, we're outdoors.

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:41.570
So again, take a look at yourself.

00:22:41.570 --> 00:22:44.680
But the typical situation that comes in

00:22:44.680 --> 00:22:45.870
that becomes a problem

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:48.970
is when the camera sees a lot of daylight,

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:51.550
just for one end of the
spectrum, the blue end,

00:22:51.550 --> 00:22:53.800
and then you have an incandescent bulb

00:22:53.800 --> 00:22:55.440
and now you look a little orange.

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:58.163
So you wanna compensate
that with the daylight bulb.

00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:02.980
Natural light versus artificial light.

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:05.360
If in the upper left hand corner,

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:07.520
wouldn't be nice that
all our Zoom meetings

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:12.200
could have nice soft light
coming in through big windows,

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:17.200
that person looks great, but
we don't all have that luxury.

00:23:17.310 --> 00:23:21.130
So the person in the lower left
has nice daylight coming in.

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:24.120
You see coming in and it's
illuminating her face.

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:27.370
In other words, it's in
front of her, not behind her.

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:29.560
We don't actually really
know what's behind her,

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:31.210
but humor me here.

00:23:31.210 --> 00:23:32.990
You have a lot of daylight coming soft,

00:23:32.990 --> 00:23:35.250
daylight illuminating your face,

00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:38.690
but then she also has a lamp on her desk

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:41.910
to help illuminate
herself a little bit more.

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:43.780
That would be a classic situation

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:45.703
where you put a daylight bulb in it.

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:51.630
Now people have decided
that they need more light

00:23:51.630 --> 00:23:53.290
when they do all their Zoom meetings.

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:55.270
So enter the ring light.

00:23:55.270 --> 00:23:58.380
A lot of people go out
and buy these ring lights.

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:01.800
They're okay unless you put it right dead

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:04.480
above the lens of your camera.

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:06.540
And then if you see the Jeff Carlson image

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:08.310
in the upper right hand corner,

00:24:08.310 --> 00:24:11.050
you start seeing reflection
of the ring light

00:24:11.050 --> 00:24:14.090
in your glasses or in your eyes,

00:24:14.090 --> 00:24:17.850
you'll see like a little circle of light.

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:21.030
The best thing to do is to put
your light off to the side.

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:24.140
My rule of thumb in
lighting, any situation,

00:24:24.140 --> 00:24:26.997
is that I always take the
light and move it one foot over

00:24:26.997 --> 00:24:29.820
and one foot above the lens.

00:24:29.820 --> 00:24:31.810
That's typically my starting point

00:24:31.810 --> 00:24:34.250
in any kind of a lighting situation.

00:24:34.250 --> 00:24:36.950
So if you're going to
have any extra lights,

00:24:36.950 --> 00:24:39.330
that would probably be
a good place to start.

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:42.113
And then you fine tune it a
little bit as you go along.

00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:46.643
What we will hear.

00:24:48.890 --> 00:24:50.730
Audio is really important.

00:24:50.730 --> 00:24:54.080
In any kind of online presentation

00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:56.170
or any kind of a recording,

00:24:56.170 --> 00:25:00.600
people will kinda forgive
not so good images,

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:03.050
but if they don't understand
the audio, you lost them,

00:25:03.050 --> 00:25:04.470
they're gone.

00:25:04.470 --> 00:25:08.100
So your audio has to be good quality.

00:25:08.100 --> 00:25:09.703
It has to be clear.

00:25:10.760 --> 00:25:14.640
The microphone, you should
have a directional microphone,

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:18.730
if you can, or earbuds or a headset.

00:25:18.730 --> 00:25:21.900
Or if you're going to use
your laptop microphone,

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:23.430
you gotta have it closer.

00:25:23.430 --> 00:25:26.293
You can't have it any more
than two to three feet away.

00:25:27.650 --> 00:25:30.853
So always remember, you
have picture and sound.

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:35.910
An iPhone, some people when
they're doing their recordings

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:37.490
will use their iPhone.

00:25:37.490 --> 00:25:41.390
Just make sure it's no more
than arms length away from you.

00:25:41.390 --> 00:25:43.650
Set it up on a bookcase or on a tripod

00:25:43.650 --> 00:25:46.310
or something like that,
so it's at eye level,

00:25:46.310 --> 00:25:50.000
and make sure it's close enough.

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:55.000
Now Zoom does have those controls
for improving your sound.

00:25:56.910 --> 00:26:00.960
I found that a USB microphone,
if you can find them,

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:02.810
actually now, they're not hard to find.

00:26:02.810 --> 00:26:04.900
When the COVID first happened,

00:26:04.900 --> 00:26:08.393
it was difficult to find one
of these USB microphones.

00:26:09.410 --> 00:26:11.570
But let's go through
a little bit of a dive

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:13.203
a little deeper into audio.

00:26:14.140 --> 00:26:18.290
The sound waves from your
voice or any kind of sound

00:26:18.290 --> 00:26:21.313
hit the diaphragm inside the microphone.

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:26.090
It vibrates and that creates
an electrical signal.

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:29.490
The closer you are to the
microphone, the better,

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:32.143
unless you're too close
and then you'll distort it.

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:36.240
Most microphones have
different pickup patterns.

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:38.480
So you have to be aware of that.

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:42.220
In the bottom picture, you
have the microphone on the left

00:26:42.220 --> 00:26:44.780
is what we call an
omnidirectional microphone.

00:26:44.780 --> 00:26:47.860
It hears equally in all directions.

00:26:47.860 --> 00:26:50.450
Whereas the second one, that's a cardioid,

00:26:50.450 --> 00:26:52.020
it's a heart shape pattern,

00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:54.620
and that picks up more directionally,

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:57.370
which is actually best in this situation.

00:26:57.370 --> 00:26:59.300
Then you have other patterns,

00:26:59.300 --> 00:27:02.210
especially in the one in the bottom right

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:06.080
in that blue window there.

00:27:06.080 --> 00:27:08.120
That's a figure eight pattern.

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:10.490
So that's wonderful for podcasts,

00:27:10.490 --> 00:27:13.050
when you have one microphone
for two people on it

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:14.083
on either side.

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:20.090
Now the picture on the right
gives you kind of an idea

00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:25.090
of what we call the fall off
or the DB or decibel pattern.

00:27:27.040 --> 00:27:30.450
You'll see that right at the
surface of the microphone,

00:27:30.450 --> 00:27:32.570
you got 25DB,

00:27:32.570 --> 00:27:36.080
but it doesn't take much for
it to fall off to like 5DB.

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:39.480
So you have a significant
loss in audio quality

00:27:40.400 --> 00:27:43.180
by not being close
enough to the microphone.

00:27:43.180 --> 00:27:47.320
So the way this manifests itself

00:27:47.320 --> 00:27:50.350
is that if you have a microphone far away,

00:27:50.350 --> 00:27:53.250
it's picking up more of the room noise,

00:27:53.250 --> 00:27:55.103
and you're gonna sound echoy,

00:27:56.540 --> 00:27:59.133
and it's not gonna be
that clear and distinct.

00:28:02.120 --> 00:28:04.680
This is a closer shot of my microphone.

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:07.253
I use a Yeti microphone here.

00:28:08.780 --> 00:28:11.860
You'll see that it does
have a gain control on it,

00:28:11.860 --> 00:28:14.940
but more importantly, it has
that little pickup pattern.

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:18.200
That's the center image there.

00:28:18.200 --> 00:28:20.640
You wanna make sure it's on that cardioid

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:23.200
or heart shaped pattern.

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:25.710
I know so many people get
themselves a microphone,

00:28:25.710 --> 00:28:29.170
and then forget to set
it where it needs to be.

00:28:29.170 --> 00:28:32.410
And then you always go
onto your Zoom Preferences

00:28:32.410 --> 00:28:35.740
and you do your test audio
and play around with it.

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:39.823
So before you do your all
important recording or meeting,

00:28:40.760 --> 00:28:42.100
test the microphone,

00:28:42.100 --> 00:28:44.630
make sure that you don't have
the mute button pushed on it,

00:28:44.630 --> 00:28:46.180
which I've done more than once.

00:28:47.765 --> 00:28:50.210
See if you can get a good clean sound

00:28:50.210 --> 00:28:54.270
where the green bar goes
about 2/3 to three quarters

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:58.890
of the way to the right
distance between me

00:28:58.890 --> 00:29:01.980
and the microphone, and
you get a good sound.

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:03.880
Now, you're ready for
your important meeting,

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:05.823
'cause people really understand you.

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:10.083
So some audio tips.

00:29:11.310 --> 00:29:13.010
Pay attention to your environment.

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:16.310
Do this when you're selecting a location

00:29:16.310 --> 00:29:17.880
to do your recording.

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:20.780
So not only is it a good background,

00:29:20.780 --> 00:29:24.150
but stop and be quiet
for a moment and listen.

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:28.320
Are you hearing some noises
that you can either turn off

00:29:28.320 --> 00:29:29.840
or you can avoid?

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:33.323
Everything from the refrigerator
that might come on to.

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:37.640
I know some rooms, you hear the pipes

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:42.510
or you hear just the
sound of the air handlers.

00:29:42.510 --> 00:29:44.483
So avoid situations like that.

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:49.490
Yeah, it is obvious, but silence
your phone and your emails

00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:50.440
and all that other stuff,

00:29:50.440 --> 00:29:51.530
because they will go off

00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:54.083
right in the middle of
your best recording.

00:29:55.180 --> 00:29:57.820
Rustling of papers, tapping on the table.

00:29:57.820 --> 00:30:00.343
I've got my little clicker over.

00:30:01.940 --> 00:30:03.990
You don't wanna have
it tapping on surfaces

00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:05.160
or setting down.

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:06.240
But rustling papers,

00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:08.820
especially if you're
reading from a script,

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:11.703
that can really be picked
up very quickly and easily.

00:30:12.810 --> 00:30:15.363
Also consider your surfaces.

00:30:16.870 --> 00:30:19.150
Sound waves bounce.

00:30:19.150 --> 00:30:23.880
So in other words, if you have
a lot of large flat surfaces,

00:30:23.880 --> 00:30:26.050
the sound waves will bounce off of that

00:30:26.050 --> 00:30:28.440
and come back into the microphone.

00:30:28.440 --> 00:30:31.060
Especially consider behind you.

00:30:31.060 --> 00:30:34.820
If you have a big flat wall
behind you, guess what happens.

00:30:34.820 --> 00:30:36.410
Your voice goes out.

00:30:36.410 --> 00:30:40.470
It gets picked up on the
microphone, bounces off of surfaces

00:30:40.470 --> 00:30:42.670
and comes back into microphone.

00:30:42.670 --> 00:30:46.360
That's what gives you
that echoy muddy sound.

00:30:46.360 --> 00:30:48.860
So always pay attention to flat surfaces.

00:30:48.860 --> 00:30:52.533
Break it up, a couple chairs
here and there, curtains,

00:30:53.430 --> 00:30:56.780
but also pay attention
to what's behind you.

00:30:56.780 --> 00:30:57.613
I know that.

00:30:59.100 --> 00:31:01.810
Believe it or not, I was
working in this industry

00:31:01.810 --> 00:31:02.730
for many years

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:06.703
and we used to make these
little sound booths.

00:31:07.772 --> 00:31:10.390
You put foam on it and you
could actually buy them,

00:31:10.390 --> 00:31:13.760
but we used to be able to do voiceovers.

00:31:13.760 --> 00:31:17.430
I remember talking to some
real rock and roll expert

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:20.300
who had done so many recordings with bands

00:31:20.300 --> 00:31:21.190
and stuff like that.

00:31:21.190 --> 00:31:25.970
I was saying, yeah, we
got this nice foam stuff

00:31:25.970 --> 00:31:27.540
and it was okay,

00:31:27.540 --> 00:31:30.410
but for some reason, the
sound still isn't great.

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:33.260
He says, "Guy, what's behind you?"

00:31:33.260 --> 00:31:36.050
He says, "the wall, you idiot.

00:31:36.050 --> 00:31:39.050
The bounce, little sound
is bouncing off the wall."

00:31:39.050 --> 00:31:40.460
Oh, sorry.

00:31:40.460 --> 00:31:43.390
So after that, we always put some curtains

00:31:43.390 --> 00:31:44.573
or something behind.

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:46.950
It's not intuitive.

00:31:46.950 --> 00:31:48.220
It's behind you.

00:31:48.220 --> 00:31:50.520
But those large flat surfaces behind you

00:31:50.520 --> 00:31:52.810
can actually be muddying your sound,

00:31:52.810 --> 00:31:54.740
because the sound waves
are bouncing off of them

00:31:54.740 --> 00:31:57.080
and hitting the microphone a little bit

00:31:57.080 --> 00:31:58.203
after your voice did.

00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:01.880
Clothing choices

00:32:04.970 --> 00:32:07.343
White, black.

00:32:09.730 --> 00:32:11.713
Certainly, black can be very slimming.

00:32:13.690 --> 00:32:16.850
But remember, what we're trying to do,

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:19.250
we're trying to show off your face.

00:32:19.250 --> 00:32:24.250
If you have a black shirt
on and the camera is saying,

00:32:25.300 --> 00:32:27.240
oh gee, I don't have enough light.

00:32:27.240 --> 00:32:30.650
Let me open up the iris,
because it's all very dark.

00:32:30.650 --> 00:32:33.230
Well, now your face is
gonna be too bright.

00:32:33.230 --> 00:32:36.590
So you have to pay attention
to your clothing, first of all,

00:32:36.590 --> 00:32:40.360
as it has to be pleasant and
something that you wanna wear,

00:32:40.360 --> 00:32:41.680
but you also wanna make sure

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:44.310
it's not giving you
some technical problems.

00:32:44.310 --> 00:32:47.910
If you have a lot of white or black,

00:32:47.910 --> 00:32:50.700
it can sometimes trick the camera's iris

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:53.640
into doing things that you
didn't really want it to do.

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:57.050
So we often recommend people
use kind of neutral clothings,

00:32:57.050 --> 00:33:02.050
pastels, off whites,
grays, browns and blues,

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:03.960
that usually works best.

00:33:03.960 --> 00:33:06.820
It's certainly easier for us
when we're lighting people,

00:33:06.820 --> 00:33:09.940
but it'll be easier for
you to adjust the camera,

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:12.820
which will automatically be adjusting.

00:33:12.820 --> 00:33:15.460
Greens are good unless
you're doing chromakey,

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:18.433
'cause most of the
chromakeys are green screens.

00:33:19.410 --> 00:33:23.070
Television, computers really
don't do reds very well,

00:33:23.070 --> 00:33:26.080
oranges or pinks.

00:33:26.080 --> 00:33:27.010
They get confused.

00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:28.670
They don't really, really know

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:30.610
how to distinguish them that well.

00:33:30.610 --> 00:33:32.990
You can wear them, but just keep in mind

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:35.580
that it is going to be a
little bit more of a challenge

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:36.413
for the camera.

00:33:38.160 --> 00:33:43.050
Avoid pin stripes, real thin
pin stripes or herringbone,

00:33:43.050 --> 00:33:44.570
any kind of a close pattern.

00:33:44.570 --> 00:33:47.300
The reason for that is
that the way the image

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:51.150
is being created is it's
scanning back and forth.

00:33:51.150 --> 00:33:53.210
The electrons are scanning back and forth,

00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:56.210
and that's how television video, whatever,

00:33:56.210 --> 00:33:57.870
is able to create an image.

00:33:57.870 --> 00:34:00.010
In that scanning back and forth,

00:34:00.010 --> 00:34:03.703
thin patterns confuse the camera.

00:34:04.860 --> 00:34:07.910
So you'll have what we
call a moire pattern.

00:34:07.910 --> 00:34:11.340
If you have a very thin
pin striper or herringbone,

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:13.680
it'll look like worms are
crawling up your shirt,

00:34:13.680 --> 00:34:14.833
which is not good.

00:34:16.270 --> 00:34:18.540
Avoid high contrast.

00:34:18.540 --> 00:34:21.530
You don't wanna be mixing black and white,

00:34:21.530 --> 00:34:24.440
'cause again, you're
thinking for the camera,

00:34:24.440 --> 00:34:26.960
and that's terrible thing is we're dealing

00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:30.340
with technology here and
we have to think for it.

00:34:30.340 --> 00:34:33.410
You have to anticipate
how it's gonna react.

00:34:33.410 --> 00:34:35.623
Jewelry, yeah, that can be an issue.

00:34:37.370 --> 00:34:42.370
But you wanna dress the
way you feel comfortable.

00:34:43.610 --> 00:34:45.640
Just take a look at it.

00:34:45.640 --> 00:34:48.430
That's kinda the rule
of thumb a lot of this.

00:34:48.430 --> 00:34:51.110
Make sure you look at yourself
before you do the recording

00:34:51.110 --> 00:34:53.810
and see if you can make an
improvement here and there.

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:59.740
But the bottom line is it's
setting you up to look good

00:34:59.740 --> 00:35:02.260
and make it visible, your eyes visible.

00:35:02.260 --> 00:35:05.090
You wanna be able to see the person,

00:35:05.090 --> 00:35:07.200
because you're the one
that's the subject matter

00:35:07.200 --> 00:35:10.350
and it's your presentation,
what you're saying

00:35:10.350 --> 00:35:13.193
and how you look that's
really selling your story.

00:35:15.380 --> 00:35:16.850
PowerPoint slides.

00:35:16.850 --> 00:35:18.000
You're gonna have them.

00:35:19.430 --> 00:35:22.240
Again, I understand with the 3MT,

00:35:22.240 --> 00:35:25.920
you're gonna be alongside
of your PowerPoint.

00:35:25.920 --> 00:35:29.990
In most of us, the Zoom puts
us in the upper hand corner,

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:31.190
upper right hand corner.

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:35.253
But you wanna make sure that
you're using wide screen.

00:35:36.230 --> 00:35:38.290
You wanna make sure that your slides

00:35:38.290 --> 00:35:41.290
are supporting your story,

00:35:41.290 --> 00:35:45.180
not replacing it or distracting people.

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:49.490
1920 by 1080, you gotta remember
this number, 1920 by 1080,

00:35:49.490 --> 00:35:52.420
which is wide screen,
it's the old 16 by 9.

00:35:52.420 --> 00:35:56.980
About 10, 15 years ago,
everything went wide screen.

00:35:56.980 --> 00:36:00.230
So the old four by three
kinda square'ish image

00:36:00.230 --> 00:36:03.350
that we used to watch on
television, that's kinda gone.

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:06.760
Unfortunately, PowerPoint
still has that as a default.

00:36:06.760 --> 00:36:07.610
So you wanna make sure

00:36:07.610 --> 00:36:09.280
that when you're creating your slides,

00:36:09.280 --> 00:36:11.634
you select wide screen.

00:36:11.634 --> 00:36:13.683
It gives you more workable area too.

00:36:15.150 --> 00:36:17.510
I find that it's better to
use a dark back background

00:36:17.510 --> 00:36:19.360
with white or light text.

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:23.630
Again, I'm adverse to
having bright white sources

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:25.980
or light sources that are distracting,

00:36:25.980 --> 00:36:28.050
because again, the rule thumb,

00:36:28.050 --> 00:36:29.970
when people are looking
at any given frame,

00:36:29.970 --> 00:36:31.740
they're gonna look at eyes first,

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:33.500
then they're gonna look for motion

00:36:33.500 --> 00:36:35.900
and then they're gonna look
for the brightest spot.

00:36:35.900 --> 00:36:40.150
So if your slide is very and
white, it's only natural,

00:36:40.150 --> 00:36:43.313
but it's gonna be a little distracting.

00:36:44.530 --> 00:36:46.410
Visibility resolution.

00:36:46.410 --> 00:36:51.200
You wanna just think about how
people are seeing your image.

00:36:51.200 --> 00:36:55.090
Are they gonna be looking at
it on a cell phone or an iPad?

00:36:55.090 --> 00:36:58.310
Sure, everything looks great
at arms length with a laptop

00:36:58.310 --> 00:36:59.450
or a computer.

00:36:59.450 --> 00:37:01.760
But what will that slide look like

00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:04.570
when it's reduced with smaller image?

00:37:04.570 --> 00:37:07.010
Again, remember your copyright.

00:37:07.010 --> 00:37:08.690
Do proper attribution.

00:37:08.690 --> 00:37:11.270
You want to not be surprised down the road

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:14.050
that you borrowed something
that you shouldn't.

00:37:14.050 --> 00:37:17.600
In this world of everything
online, still lives there,

00:37:17.600 --> 00:37:20.290
people can find it and
they'll be not happy

00:37:20.290 --> 00:37:23.133
if you just go ahead and use
their stuff without asking.

00:37:25.980 --> 00:37:28.600
Just to help illustrate the PowerPoint.

00:37:28.600 --> 00:37:30.730
Not every presentation you do

00:37:30.730 --> 00:37:33.550
will always be seen at arms length.

00:37:33.550 --> 00:37:38.130
It may be on a screen in a
room, in a conference room.

00:37:38.130 --> 00:37:42.090
So think about how people
in the back of the room

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:43.450
are gonna be seeing it.

00:37:43.450 --> 00:37:46.423
If you have a lot of white on your slide,

00:37:47.350 --> 00:37:50.170
it's gonna be difficult for, you know.

00:37:50.170 --> 00:37:52.627
Here, I'm talking about how
the camera would adjust for,

00:37:52.627 --> 00:37:54.650
but also for people.

00:37:54.650 --> 00:37:56.130
If you have a lot of white,

00:37:56.130 --> 00:38:00.590
your eyes are like adjusting
for the room and the people,

00:38:00.590 --> 00:38:03.470
and now the images are kinda blown out.

00:38:03.470 --> 00:38:06.770
Whereas if you kind of
adjust to your camera

00:38:06.770 --> 00:38:10.580
to see a slide that has a lot of white,

00:38:10.580 --> 00:38:12.720
everything else is gonna
be a little darker.

00:38:12.720 --> 00:38:16.140
So again, this is another plug
for putting darker slides,

00:38:16.140 --> 00:38:19.113
darker background slides with light text.

00:38:22.350 --> 00:38:25.090
Only natural, a lot of
logos and other images

00:38:25.090 --> 00:38:26.680
that you have are on white.

00:38:26.680 --> 00:38:30.323
So you can crop them and then
drop them in as appropriate.

00:38:31.190 --> 00:38:33.940
This is in a another way,
just use your Photoshop

00:38:33.940 --> 00:38:36.703
or any of your software to just crop it.

00:38:38.620 --> 00:38:42.533
So now let's talk about the
3MT, three minute thesis.

00:38:44.900 --> 00:38:47.640
Since you have a very short amount of time

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:48.900
to tell your story,

00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:52.500
obviously, you have to decide
what is the need to know

00:38:52.500 --> 00:38:53.733
from the nice to know.

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:56.840
This is where making that objective,

00:38:56.840 --> 00:38:59.750
what do you want people
to really walk away with

00:38:59.750 --> 00:39:02.610
at the end of your three minutes?

00:39:02.610 --> 00:39:06.500
So your visuals are going
to amplify your story,

00:39:06.500 --> 00:39:09.920
but keep in mind that if
you've got a complicated slide

00:39:09.920 --> 00:39:12.560
or you tell people to look at the slide,

00:39:12.560 --> 00:39:15.330
they're not gonna be hearing
your voice quite as much.

00:39:15.330 --> 00:39:20.330
Again, we assimilate a picture
or sound, not both equally.

00:39:21.470 --> 00:39:24.560
If you're gonna be in
the frame with the slides

00:39:24.560 --> 00:39:27.010
and anticipate, and
again, in this situation,

00:39:27.010 --> 00:39:28.730
you're gonna be to the left,

00:39:28.730 --> 00:39:31.293
you're gonna be alone
the side of the image.

00:39:32.810 --> 00:39:35.900
I find that it's better to initiate,

00:39:35.900 --> 00:39:37.700
you make your first contact.

00:39:37.700 --> 00:39:41.220
Remember, your first 15 seconds,
you're drawing people in.

00:39:41.220 --> 00:39:45.370
Talk a little bit, refer to
the slide and then back to you,

00:39:45.370 --> 00:39:48.610
so that people know when
they should look at the slide

00:39:48.610 --> 00:39:52.780
instead of being distracted,
and then come back to you

00:39:52.780 --> 00:39:56.410
and make sure that your
important points are being heard

00:39:56.410 --> 00:39:57.420
with their full attention

00:39:57.420 --> 00:39:59.840
and not kind of a distracted attention

00:39:59.840 --> 00:40:01.740
where they're looking at the
slides and looking at you

00:40:01.740 --> 00:40:04.613
and what do they really
want to concentrate on.

00:40:06.770 --> 00:40:09.200
Obviously, if you can
trim out some portions

00:40:09.200 --> 00:40:11.120
that are not needed,

00:40:11.120 --> 00:40:13.900
but most importantly,
you wanna be dynamic.

00:40:13.900 --> 00:40:18.060
You wanna bring that story to
life and give yourself time,

00:40:18.060 --> 00:40:20.050
so that you're not rushing it.

00:40:20.050 --> 00:40:23.750
Maybe that should be a two and
a half minute thesis planned

00:40:23.750 --> 00:40:25.633
to fill in for three minutes.

00:40:27.140 --> 00:40:29.460
So here's a few examples.

00:40:29.460 --> 00:40:31.530
If you notice the framing,

00:40:31.530 --> 00:40:34.290
the way you're gonna be
recording this is on Panopto,

00:40:34.290 --> 00:40:37.470
so that's gonna put you along the side.

00:40:37.470 --> 00:40:39.810
Unfortunately, it's gonna
leave a lot of dead space

00:40:39.810 --> 00:40:41.300
in the top and bottom,

00:40:41.300 --> 00:40:44.310
which is all the more reason
why you wanna make sure

00:40:44.310 --> 00:40:47.110
that your slide is not too cluttered.

00:40:47.110 --> 00:40:50.020
In this situation, it
was very dynamic speaker.

00:40:50.020 --> 00:40:53.560
He framed himself such that
you could see his hands

00:40:53.560 --> 00:40:56.560
and that's okay if
you're gonna be dynamic.

00:40:56.560 --> 00:40:59.930
The background is a little
plain, it's not bad,

00:40:59.930 --> 00:41:03.810
but when you have a
background that simple,

00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:06.040
you gotta make sure you have
enough light on your face,

00:41:06.040 --> 00:41:08.900
because you're gonna
have reflected light too.

00:41:08.900 --> 00:41:13.480
But this fellow had broke his
slides into logical patterns.

00:41:13.480 --> 00:41:16.910
So he talked, he referred to
the first part of his slide,

00:41:16.910 --> 00:41:19.000
then back to him and then to the middle

00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:20.860
and then to the latter part.

00:41:20.860 --> 00:41:24.870
So he kinda broke his slides
up into a logical fashion,

00:41:24.870 --> 00:41:28.430
and then his presentation
referred to it and then back.

00:41:28.430 --> 00:41:29.723
It was very successful.

00:41:32.030 --> 00:41:34.350
This person did a very similar type thing.

00:41:34.350 --> 00:41:37.250
You noticed that her
framing is much closer,

00:41:37.250 --> 00:41:39.540
much easier to see her eyes.

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:42.800
She wasn't one that was
gesturing that much,

00:41:42.800 --> 00:41:46.520
but her slides also were
very logically laid out.

00:41:46.520 --> 00:41:48.970
It was easy for the eye
to follow those things

00:41:48.970 --> 00:41:50.690
and then come back to her,

00:41:50.690 --> 00:41:54.213
and not be stuck on the slide
trying to figure it out.

00:41:55.630 --> 00:41:57.883
It was uncluttered,
which it was really nice.

00:42:00.020 --> 00:42:03.150
This image, okay, I'm
gonna kinda toss it to you.

00:42:03.150 --> 00:42:04.773
What do you think about this?

00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:09.250
Right.

00:42:09.250 --> 00:42:12.280
His camera level is a little low.

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:13.580
So we're seeing a ceiling.

00:42:14.946 --> 00:42:17.680
The background is a little
clutter, but not bad.

00:42:17.680 --> 00:42:20.580
I don't think this one really is that bad,

00:42:20.580 --> 00:42:22.210
but I think the most important thing

00:42:22.210 --> 00:42:24.970
is to get the camera at eye level.

00:42:24.970 --> 00:42:26.213
You wanna do that.

00:42:27.430 --> 00:42:29.670
He still would've been fairly well lit,

00:42:29.670 --> 00:42:33.050
but seeing the light fixture up above him

00:42:33.050 --> 00:42:34.400
is gonna confuse the camera.

00:42:34.400 --> 00:42:37.380
So I think if he had gotten
himself at eye level,

00:42:37.380 --> 00:42:40.780
everything else kind of
would've taken care of itself.

00:42:40.780 --> 00:42:41.940
But it also gives you,

00:42:41.940 --> 00:42:44.420
is this a situation
where you would wanna use

00:42:44.420 --> 00:42:48.520
one of those virtual backgrounds
or the soft focus ones?

00:42:48.520 --> 00:42:50.690
I think those are fine.

00:42:50.690 --> 00:42:53.693
It keeps you from being
distracted by the background.

00:42:54.800 --> 00:42:57.630
The only thing that
you get to keep in mind

00:42:57.630 --> 00:42:59.770
in using the virtual backgrounds

00:42:59.770 --> 00:43:03.330
is to make sure it's a clean,
what we call a clean mask.

00:43:03.330 --> 00:43:06.450
In other words, you don't
see any residual image.

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:08.240
As the person, you have to move your head.

00:43:08.240 --> 00:43:11.400
You've probably seen it if
you used a virtual background.

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:15.080
When you move a little
bit, sometimes the camera,

00:43:15.080 --> 00:43:17.100
the technology doesn't continue

00:43:17.100 --> 00:43:19.960
making a nice clean image around you.

00:43:19.960 --> 00:43:23.070
It's kind of a like soft and
sometimes you'll actually see

00:43:23.070 --> 00:43:24.990
a little bit of the background.

00:43:24.990 --> 00:43:27.950
So try things out, in a
circumstance like this,

00:43:27.950 --> 00:43:31.330
You could argue that aside
from getting at eye level,

00:43:31.330 --> 00:43:33.120
you could argue that maybe the background

00:43:33.120 --> 00:43:34.190
is a little too busy

00:43:34.190 --> 00:43:37.530
and you wanna go with the
soft focus virtual background,

00:43:37.530 --> 00:43:39.453
or you could just go with what it is.

00:43:40.568 --> 00:43:43.610
He's slides, again,
you're actually looking

00:43:43.610 --> 00:43:46.333
at the eyes of the zebra
for a second there too.

00:43:48.040 --> 00:43:51.090
So make sure your slides
are easily visible.

00:43:51.090 --> 00:43:53.000
You're not wasting any space,

00:43:53.000 --> 00:43:54.703
because in a situation like this,

00:43:55.730 --> 00:44:00.730
your slide is only about 1/3
of the usable entire screen.

00:44:03.420 --> 00:44:05.333
So playing accordingly.

00:44:08.560 --> 00:44:12.053
Going back to images and legal
issues and stuff like that.

00:44:12.920 --> 00:44:15.820
Since I do have a JED, I have
to think about these things.

00:44:16.710 --> 00:44:19.410
You wanna be careful of your copyright

00:44:19.410 --> 00:44:22.000
and make sure you get
permissions and all that stuff.

00:44:22.000 --> 00:44:23.130
In the old saying,

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:25.280
if you do it right,
you can sleep at night.

00:44:26.780 --> 00:44:30.810
When I upload videos to
the Yale YouTube channel,

00:44:30.810 --> 00:44:35.810
I am continuously amazed
that within a minute,

00:44:37.540 --> 00:44:40.070
sometimes seconds,

00:44:40.070 --> 00:44:42.380
I will know whether
there was a legal issue

00:44:42.380 --> 00:44:43.810
and there's a copyright issue.

00:44:43.810 --> 00:44:45.053
Usually, with music.

00:44:46.370 --> 00:44:48.640
Sometimes I'll get the
material and we edit it

00:44:48.640 --> 00:44:50.870
and we put it up, and we don't
realize that the presenter

00:44:50.870 --> 00:44:52.600
had something in it.

00:44:52.600 --> 00:44:57.180
But on YouTube and any
of these online things,

00:44:57.180 --> 00:45:00.200
they were able to pick up misused images

00:45:00.200 --> 00:45:03.060
and certainly misused sound.

00:45:03.060 --> 00:45:03.970
So you wanna be careful.

00:45:03.970 --> 00:45:06.730
It's as simple as sending
an email to the person

00:45:06.730 --> 00:45:09.533
that you're using their images
and just ask permission.

00:45:11.010 --> 00:45:12.733
Speaker permission forms.

00:45:13.583 --> 00:45:16.400
The rule of thumb is that if
you're using somebody's name

00:45:16.400 --> 00:45:20.021
in likeness, they need to give permission.

00:45:20.021 --> 00:45:23.100
So you may have been asked
to sign a release form

00:45:23.100 --> 00:45:24.670
for your 3MTs,

00:45:24.670 --> 00:45:27.743
or any other time that
you're using a guest speaker.

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:32.500
Alternatively, in some
cases, you can put a notice

00:45:32.500 --> 00:45:35.640
at the beginning that says
the event is being recorded

00:45:35.640 --> 00:45:38.270
and it gives you an option out.

00:45:38.270 --> 00:45:41.610
Or when you option in, you
know that you're being recorded

00:45:41.610 --> 00:45:43.253
and it maybe become public.

00:45:44.253 --> 00:45:46.980
Yes, your presentation
may be seen on YouTube.

00:45:46.980 --> 00:45:51.780
All of you, if that's one
of the places that material

00:45:51.780 --> 00:45:53.533
that we you do goes.

00:45:54.580 --> 00:45:57.760
The Yale Office of General
Counsel has a website.

00:45:57.760 --> 00:46:00.930
They do have some legal
information and some guidance,

00:46:00.930 --> 00:46:04.440
and they have something
called a Fair Use tool.

00:46:04.440 --> 00:46:06.900
If you have questions about

00:46:06.900 --> 00:46:11.900
whether what you're using
fulfills the exclusion

00:46:12.400 --> 00:46:14.960
from copyright for what's called fair use,

00:46:14.960 --> 00:46:18.960
they have a tool and you
could just look at it.

00:46:18.960 --> 00:46:22.930
Basically, fair use is a
four point balancing test

00:46:22.930 --> 00:46:26.470
where you're looking at
different elements of the image

00:46:26.470 --> 00:46:29.270
and balancing it to see
whether it tips the balance

00:46:29.270 --> 00:46:31.770
to, yeah, that's fair
use, go ahead and use it,

00:46:31.770 --> 00:46:33.960
to that's not really fair use,

00:46:33.960 --> 00:46:35.770
you're probably gonna get in trouble.

00:46:35.770 --> 00:46:37.633
So that's one thing to consider.

00:46:41.250 --> 00:46:43.480
Bottom line, you're gonna
successfully communicate

00:46:43.480 --> 00:46:45.410
your story on this, right?

00:46:45.410 --> 00:46:47.970
When you're online,
you're telling a story.

00:46:47.970 --> 00:46:52.390
Keep in mind that people can
assimilate the spoken word

00:46:52.390 --> 00:46:56.410
or your images, but not
necessarily at the same time,

00:46:56.410 --> 00:46:59.420
certainly not equally at the same time.

00:46:59.420 --> 00:47:03.193
The video resolution is important.

00:47:04.030 --> 00:47:08.103
Many people do look at things
on cellphones or iPads.

00:47:09.260 --> 00:47:10.990
Just keep in mind.

00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:14.100
I told you to remember the 1920 by 1080,

00:47:14.100 --> 00:47:17.623
that's your full resolution
for high definition.

00:47:18.460 --> 00:47:21.387
It may be squeezed down to 1280 by 720,

00:47:21.387 --> 00:47:25.470
and it's still your 16 by 9 aspect ratio.

00:47:25.470 --> 00:47:27.580
But if things get squeezed down,

00:47:27.580 --> 00:47:31.710
is your slide gonna be so
detailed that we don't see it,

00:47:31.710 --> 00:47:33.790
or it's gonna be so detailed that people

00:47:33.790 --> 00:47:35.930
are spending their time
trying to figure it out

00:47:35.930 --> 00:47:38.240
and they're not paying attention to you.

00:47:38.240 --> 00:47:40.000
So again, that's a plug for making sure

00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:42.633
that your slides are not cluttered.

00:47:44.260 --> 00:47:46.130
Remember about the slides

00:47:46.130 --> 00:47:50.573
is they should be supporting
your important spoken points.

00:47:51.560 --> 00:47:54.750
Your spoken word is
going to carry the day.

00:47:54.750 --> 00:47:57.270
That's gonna decide
whether you are successful

00:47:57.270 --> 00:47:59.370
in communicating your story or not.

00:47:59.370 --> 00:48:02.820
The slides will help,
but it's your spoken word

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:04.620
that's going to make the difference.

00:48:05.610 --> 00:48:07.550
If you're on a slide,

00:48:07.550 --> 00:48:09.670
your voice is reinforcing the visuals.

00:48:09.670 --> 00:48:11.820
In other words, if you've
called people's attention

00:48:11.820 --> 00:48:15.410
to the slide and you want
them to be looking at it,

00:48:15.410 --> 00:48:18.030
keep in mind that they're
gonna now be focused on that

00:48:18.030 --> 00:48:19.670
and they're gonna be reading it,

00:48:19.670 --> 00:48:21.910
and your voice is not so important.

00:48:21.910 --> 00:48:23.470
So that's not the time

00:48:23.470 --> 00:48:26.240
when you make your most important point.

00:48:26.240 --> 00:48:28.490
You wanna get people back to you

00:48:28.490 --> 00:48:30.873
and then you can make your point.

00:48:32.000 --> 00:48:34.780
But above all, you're telling a story

00:48:35.720 --> 00:48:38.223
with your voice and your visuals.

00:48:41.230 --> 00:48:45.350
Hopefully, that helped you
get your online presentation

00:48:45.350 --> 00:48:47.320
in your staging in order.

00:48:47.320 --> 00:48:49.043
Don't forget the evaluation form.

